
# ── Journey 2: Everyday Conversations ──────────────────────────────────────
levels.append({'id':11,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Discussing Current Events',
'scenario':'You are at a casual Friday afternoon lunch with three work colleagues. Someone mentions a major news story that broke this morning. The conversation could easily become heated given the divisive nature of the topic. You want to engage thoughtfully, share a perspective, and keep the atmosphere collegial and positive.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'A colleague mentions the headline and looks to the group for reactions. How do you enter the conversation without immediately inflaming things?',
'Acknowledge the story briefly and say, "It\'s definitely a big one — what\'s your read on what\'s actually happening?"',
['Launch immediately into your strongly held opinion on who is to blame.','Say, "I try not to follow the news — it\'s all too depressing."','Change the subject entirely by asking what everyone is ordering.'],
'Acknowledging the story and inviting their perspective first gives you information about the room\'s temperature before you commit to a position.',
'Immediately asserting blame can polarise the table. Dismissing news entirely can seem disengaged. Changing the subject abruptly when someone raised it feels like avoidance.'),
mcq(2,'A colleague shares a strong opinion about the story. You disagree with parts of it. How do you respond?',
'"That\'s an interesting angle — I see it slightly differently. For me, what stands out is..." and then share your view calmly.',
['"That is completely wrong — here is why..." and list your counterpoints.','Nod and agree even though you disagree, to keep the peace.','Say, "I don\'t really know enough about it to say."'],
'Acknowledging their view before presenting yours signals respect and keeps the exchange feeling like a dialogue rather than a debate.',
'Immediately declaring them wrong escalates tension. False agreement is dishonest and weakens trust. Claiming ignorance when you have a view is a missed opportunity for meaningful exchange.'),
mcq(3,'The conversation starts getting animated, with two colleagues talking over each other. How do you help de-escalate while staying engaged?',
'Wait for a pause, then say, "There\'s clearly a lot of different angles here — I think we might actually agree on more than we think. What\'s the common thread?"',
['Join the talking-over and assert your view more loudly.','Go quiet and stare at your food until it passes.','Say, "Okay, let\'s not talk politics at lunch — it always ends badly."'],
'Identifying potential common ground and inviting the group to find it is a skilled de-escalation move that keeps everyone engaged and respected.',
'Talking louder than two people already talking over each other adds fuel. Going silent contributes nothing and may feel like abandonment. Blanket bans on topics can feel condescending.'),
mcq(4,'The group finds a point of agreement on one aspect of the story and the mood lightens. How do you reinforce this positive shift?',
'"See — that\'s actually where I think most people land when you cut through the noise. It\'s the framing that makes it seem more divisive than it is."',
['Immediately pivot to another controversial topic to keep the energy going.','Say, "I knew we\'d agree eventually — I was right, wasn\'t I?"','Stay quiet since the group seems to have found their way without you.'],
'Affirming the shared ground and offering a gentle observation about framing consolidates the positive shift and helps the group feel good about the conversation.',
'Pivoting to another hot topic risks undoing the calm you just restored. Claiming credit for their agreement is patronising. Disengaging at the moment of resolution misses the chance to close well.'),
mcq(5,'As you head back to the office, a colleague says, "That was actually a really good conversation — usually these go badly." How do you respond?',
'"Agreed — I think as long as we\'re genuinely curious about each other\'s views rather than trying to win, it works."',
['"Yeah, I was ready for it to get ugly — glad it didn\'t."','"Thanks — I made sure to steer it before it went off the rails."','"I know, right? You and Marcus were really going at it for a minute!"'],
'Offering a thoughtful observation about what made the conversation work turns the moment into a learning for the whole group.',
'Focusing on how close it came to going badly emphasises the near-miss rather than the success. Taking sole credit for steering it is self-congratulatory. Pointing out who was getting heated can embarrass that person.')],
'response':resp('You navigated a potentially divisive current events conversation with curiosity, balance, and a light hand on the de-escalation. Now type or say aloud how you would frame this topic if you were briefly summarising the conversation to a colleague who missed lunch.',
'Excellent! Discussing current events well is a skill — it requires genuine curiosity, comfort with disagreement, and the ability to find common ground. You showed all three today.')})

levels.append({'id':12,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Talking About Hobbies',
'scenario':'You are at a social gathering where you find yourself seated next to someone you have never met — Sam. The conversation has turned toward personal interests and hobbies. Sam mentions they have recently taken up landscape photography. You want to connect genuinely, whether or not you share the same interest.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Sam mentions they\'ve taken up landscape photography. How do you open the hobby conversation in a way that invites them to share more?',
'Oh that\'s great — what got you into it? Was there a specific moment or trip that sparked it?',
['Nice — I\'m more of an indoor person myself, so I don\'t really get the appeal.','Have you ever considered switching to portrait photography? It\'s more versatile.','Cool! My cousin is really into photography too — let me tell you about her work.'],
'Asking about the origin story of a hobby invites a personal and often passionate response. It signals that you see the hobby as meaningful rather than just a fact.',
'Announcing you do not share the appeal closes the conversation down. Immediately suggesting they change hobbies is unwelcoming. Pivoting to your cousin\'s photography redirects the conversation away from Sam before it has started.'),
mcq(2,'Sam shares that they started after a trip to Iceland that left them wanting to capture what they saw. How do you keep the energy going?',
'Iceland — that must have been stunning. What kind of shots do you try to capture? Is it all about the light, or is it more about finding the unexpected angle?',
['Oh, I went to Iceland two years ago — the food was amazing, wasn\'t it?','So do you use a professional camera or just your phone?','Wow, that sounds expensive. Photography gear is really pricey, right?'],
'Asking a specific, layered question about their craft shows you are genuinely curious and invites them to share the technical and creative side of their passion.',
'Redirecting to your own Iceland trip takes the focus off their photography story. Asking about their camera gear before understanding their passion turns the conversation technical too soon. Commenting on cost frames the hobby as a financial burden.'),
mcq(3,'Sam opens up about how they love golden hour shooting and shows you a photo on their phone. It is genuinely beautiful. How do you respond?',
'This is really striking — the way the light catches the texture of the ground here is incredible. You clearly have a great eye for it.',
['Nice! Have you ever thought about selling these?','Wow — did you use any filters on this or is it straight out of camera?','It\'s lovely! My friend is a professional photographer — she\'d probably have great tips for you.'],
'Responding to the specific visual quality of the image — noting the light and texture — shows you looked at it carefully. This is exactly the kind of specific appreciation that means something.',
'Jumping to commercialising their hobby before they\'ve even expressed that interest can feel reductive. Asking about filters right away can feel like you\'re looking for a reason the photo isn\'t "real." Offering your friend\'s tips is well-meaning but slightly dismissive.'),
mcq(4,'Sam asks if you have any hobbies. You enjoy cooking but worry it seems less impressive than landscape photography. How do you share it confidently?',
'I love cooking — it\'s my equivalent of what you described: something that takes me out of my head and into a completely different kind of focus.',
['Nothing as cool as photography — just boring old cooking.','I cook, but nothing worth talking about really.','I cook! Maybe not as creative as photography, but it pays off at dinner.'],
'Drawing a parallel between your hobby and theirs — the shared sense of focus and absorption — creates connection and frames your interest with genuine pride.',
'Calling your own hobby "boring" or "nothing worth talking about" sells yourself short and can create an awkward pause. The "pays off at dinner" quip is light but deflects from genuine sharing.'),
mcq(5,'Sam says this has been one of the most enjoyable conversations they\'ve had in a while and that your cooking parallel really resonated. How do you close the hobby conversation?',
'That means a lot — I think hobbies reveal so much about how people actually think. You\'ve made me want to look up Iceland photography trips now.',
['Ha — I\'m just a good talker!','Suggesting a cook-off and photo exhibition someday, then laughing it off without any real follow-through.','Same. I should go back to circulating though — talk later maybe.'],
'Affirming what you appreciated about the exchange and expressing genuine curiosity sparked by their passion leaves the conversation feeling complete and memorable.',
'Crediting your general social skill deflects the genuine connection you both built. A jokey collaboration idea without follow-through can feel hollow. Saying you should circulate right after they expressed warmth feels like a dismissal.')],
'response':resp('You built a genuine connection with Sam through curiosity about their hobby and confident sharing of your own. Now type or say aloud the follow-up message you would send Sam a few days later referencing something specific from your conversation about photography or cooking.',
'Wonderful! Talking about hobbies well is about more than listing interests — it\'s about finding the emotional and creative core of what someone loves. You did that beautifully.')})

levels.append({'id':13,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Sharing Personal Stories',
'scenario':'You are at a relaxed evening dinner with close friends. The conversation has shifted toward a topic close to your heart — a significant personal challenge you overcame in the past year. You want to share the story in a way that is authentic and connects with the group, without oversharing or making it feel like a dramatic monologue.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'The conversation has naturally reached a point where sharing your story feels appropriate. How do you begin?',
'"Actually, this past year taught me something I didn\'t expect — can I share something that happened?" and wait for their nod.',
['Launch straight into the full story without checking whether people are ready to listen.','Say, "I don\'t want to make this about me — forget I said anything."','Start with, "You probably won\'t relate to this, but..." as a disclaimer.'],
'Signalling that you want to share and briefly pausing for consent respects the group\'s conversational flow and frames your story as something you are choosing to offer, not imposing.',
'Launching without a bridge can feel abrupt. Backing away entirely when the moment is right wastes a genuine opportunity. Prefacing with "you won\'t relate" undermines connection before the story even begins.'),
mcq(2,'You have started sharing the story. Midway through you notice people are genuinely engaged. How do you continue in a way that keeps the story purposeful?',
'Stay in the narrative arc — describe what happened, what you felt, what you did, and where it led you — without over-explaining or editorialising every detail.',
['Pause to say, "Am I going on too long? I can stop if this is boring."','Add a side tangent about a separate event that loosely relates.','Speed up to get to the point quickly because you don\'t want to take up too much time.'],
'Trusting the arc of the story and staying in it is what holds attention. The group\'s engaged body language tells you the pacing is working — honour that.',
'Checking whether you are boring people when they are clearly engaged is an unnecessary disruption. A side tangent breaks the narrative momentum. Speeding up when the story has natural weight rushes the audience out of the emotional moment.'),
mcq(3,'You reach the most vulnerable part of the story — the moment when things felt hardest. How do you handle this emotionally real moment?',
'Speak honestly and briefly about what you felt, without performing the emotion or downplaying it: "Honestly, I didn\'t know if I was going to be okay with how it turned out."',
['Skip over this part quickly with a joke to lighten the mood.','Overplay the emotion dramatically to make sure people feel the gravity.','Say, "I won\'t go into details — it\'s too personal."'],
'Honest, measured vulnerability at the peak of a story creates a genuine moment of connection. You do not need to perform it or protect people from it — just be present in it.',
'A joke right at the emotional peak deflects just when the story needs authenticity. Overplaying the emotion can feel theatrical and push people away. Refusing to share the key moment after introducing the story is frustrating.'),
mcq(4,'You have shared how the challenge resolved. A friend asks what you learned from it. How do you answer in a way that feels genuine rather than rehearsed?',
'"Honestly, the biggest thing was realising how much I\'d been avoiding rather than dealing with — and that it\'s never as catastrophic to face it as the avoidance makes it seem."',
['"Everything happens for a reason, I guess — you just have to trust the process."','"I don\'t know if I\'ve fully processed it yet, so I\'ll let you know when I do."','"That I\'m stronger than I thought — which I know is what everyone says."'],
'A specific, personal insight — even if imperfectly articulated — is far more connecting than a polished lesson. The specificity of "avoiding rather than dealing with" is exactly what makes it feel true.',
'"Everything happens for a reason" is a platitude that tells people nothing about you. Deferring the insight feels evasive at the close of a personal story. Pre-emptively calling your own insight cliche undercuts it before it lands.'),
mcq(5,'A friend shares that your story reminded them of something they went through. How do you respond in a way that honours their openness?',
'"Please share — I\'d love to hear it. These conversations are rare and I think we need them more."',
['"Oh interesting — but actually there\'s one more thing about my story I forgot to mention."','Say "I know exactly what you mean" and redirect back to yourself.','"You don\'t have to share if you don\'t want to — no pressure."'],
'Welcoming their story warmly and naming the value of the conversation as a whole elevates the moment from personal monologue to genuine mutual exchange.',
'Returning to your own story when someone is reaching out to share theirs is a missed opportunity for real connection. "I know exactly what you mean" and redirecting back to yourself is similar. Offering "no pressure" is well-intentioned but slightly deflecting — they already indicated they wanted to share.')],
'response':resp('You shared a personal story with authenticity, stayed in the narrative arc, handled the vulnerable moment with honesty, and opened the door for others to share. Now type or say aloud how you would close the evening — a brief message to the group acknowledging what the conversation meant to you.',
'Beautifully done! Sharing personal stories well is an act of courage and craft. You balanced honesty with restraint, vulnerability with purposeful narrative, and finished by making space for others — that is storytelling at its best.')})

levels.append({'id':14,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Giving and Receiving Compliments',
'scenario':'You are in the office on a Friday afternoon. A colleague named Ravi has just given a particularly impressive client presentation that you genuinely admired. Later that same day, your manager tells you that your own written report this week was outstanding. You need to navigate both giving and receiving a compliment with grace.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You want to compliment Ravi on his presentation. How do you do it in a way that feels genuine and specific rather than generic?',
'"Ravi, that section where you pre-empted the client\'s objection before they even raised it was really smart — I haven\'t seen that done that smoothly before."',
['"Great presentation, Ravi! Well done!"','"You always do a good job with these things, Ravi."','"Honestly, I wasn\'t sure how that would go but it turned out really well!"'],
'Specific praise — naming the exact moment and why it stood out — shows you were paying attention and that the compliment is earned rather than routine.',
'Generic praise like "well done" is pleasant but forgettable. "You always do a good job" is a generalisation that tells him nothing new. Mentioning you weren\'t sure how it would go undercuts the compliment.'),
mcq(2,'Ravi thanks you and seems genuinely pleased. He then says he was so nervous going in. How do you respond in a way that acknowledges his experience?',
'"You absolutely could not tell — which makes it even more impressive. Nervous energy channelled that well is a real skill."',
['"I could tell you were a little nervous at the start, but you recovered well."','"Nerves are normal — everyone gets them. You\'ll be fine next time too."','"Really? You seemed very confident to me. I don\'t believe you!"'],
'Validating that his nerves were invisible and reframing them as channelled energy elevates the compliment and makes the praise even more meaningful to him.',
'Confirming you noticed his nervousness, even with a "you recovered" qualifier, partially undercuts the compliment. Reassuring him about "next time" implies this was a test. Playfully not believing him makes light of his genuine disclosure.'),
mcq(3,'Later, your manager tells you your report was outstanding. How do you receive this compliment gracefully?',
'"Thank you — that\'s really good to hear. I spent a lot of time on the structure and wasn\'t sure if it landed the way I intended."',
['"Oh, I\'m sure it wasn\'t that special — I just put a few things together."','"Thank you! Yes, I did work really hard on it — honestly it\'s probably my best work yet."','"Oh good — I was actually going to ask if it was okay, so I\'m glad you said something!"'],
'Accepting the compliment, thanking them genuinely, and briefly sharing your investment in the work is the perfect balance — you honour their praise without deflecting or overclaiming.',
'Downplaying "just put a few things together" rejects their judgment. Immediately ranking it your best work ever overclaims. Saying you were about to ask if it was okay reframes the compliment as validation you needed rather than deserved.'),
mcq(4,'Your manager then asks if there\'s anything that would have made it even stronger. How do you answer this follow-up honestly?',
'"Probably the executive summary — I think I could have made the key takeaway sharper in the first paragraph so it lands faster for a busy reader."',
['"Honestly I think it was pretty complete — I\'m not sure I\'d change much."','"Maybe everything, honestly — I always think I could do better."','"I\'m not sure — what did you think was the weakest part?"'],
'Offering a specific, thoughtful self-assessment shows professional self-awareness and signals that you actively reflect on your own work — which is what strong performers do.',
'Saying you would not change much sounds defensive of work that was just complimented. Self-deprecating "everything could be better" gives no useful information. Deflecting back to them with a question dodges the invitation for genuine self-reflection.'),
mcq(5,'Your manager says, "I completely agree on the summary — great insight." How do you close this exchange in a way that leaves a strong impression?',
'"Thanks — I\'ll make that my focus on the next one. And thanks again for the feedback; it\'s genuinely useful to know what\'s landing."',
['"Great — so does this mean I\'m up for a pay rise?" and laugh.','\"Thank you so much — you\'ve made my whole week!\"','"I\'ll try to do better next time."'],
'Committing to a specific improvement and thanking them for useful feedback closes the exchange professionally and signals a growth mindset — exactly the impression you want to leave.',
'A pay rise joke, even lighthearted, pivots the conversation to negotiation in an awkward way. "You\'ve made my whole week" can sound over-dependent on external validation. "Try to do better next time" implies the current work was not good when it just was.')],
'response':resp('You gave a specific, genuine compliment to a colleague and received professional praise with grace and self-awareness. Now type or say aloud a short message you would send Ravi at the end of the day, reinforcing the compliment with one additional observation you noticed about his presentation.',
'Excellent! Giving and receiving compliments well is a social art. Specificity transforms a compliment from polite noise into something truly meaningful — and you demonstrated that clearly on both sides today.')})

levels.append({'id':15,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Phone and Video Call Etiquette',
'scenario':'You have a scheduled video call with a new client — a senior director named Helen at a company you are hoping to partner with. This is the first call and it matters. You want to present yourself professionally, manage the technology smoothly, and make the conversation feel warm and personal despite the digital medium.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You join the video call two minutes early. Helen is already there. How do you open?',
'"Helen! Great to finally put a face to the name — thanks for making time. How is your morning going?"',
['Wait in silence for her to speak first since she is the senior person.','Say, "Hi there — just let me sort my audio, one second..." and fidget with settings.','Say, "Hello! Can you hear me okay? Is my video working?"'],
'Opening warmly with her name, expressing genuine enthusiasm about connecting, and asking a light personal question sets a human, professional tone immediately.',
'Waiting in silence creates an awkward start. Troubleshooting audio live signals poor preparation. Starting with technical check-in questions focuses on the technology rather than the person.'),
mcq(2,'Helen mentions she had back-to-back calls all morning before yours. How do you acknowledge this before diving into business?',
'"That sounds like quite a morning — I appreciate you keeping this one on. I\'ll make sure we cover what matters and don\'t run over time."',
['"Oh, should we reschedule? I don\'t want to catch you at a bad time."','"Don\'t worry — this one will be the best part of your day!" and laugh loudly.','Move straight into the agenda without acknowledging what she said.'],
'Acknowledging her busy morning and committing to respecting her time shows emotional intelligence and immediately builds goodwill.',
'Offering to reschedule when she has showed up undermines confidence in the meeting. An overly jovial remark can feel performative. Ignoring what she shared and diving straight into agenda feels tone-deaf.'),
mcq(3,'Midway through the call, your internet lags and your audio cuts out for a few seconds. Helen says she lost you there. How do you handle this professionally?',
'"Apologies — my connection dropped briefly. I was saying [repeat the point clearly]. Does that come through okay now?"',
['"Oh gosh, I\'m so sorry — my internet is terrible today, I should have tested it earlier."','Repeat everything from the beginning of your point very slowly as if she couldn\'t understand.','Say "Can you hear me? Hello? Can you hear me?" until the issue resolves.'],
'A brief apology, an immediate clear restatement of your point, and a quick check-in moves the conversation forward without dwelling on the interruption.',
'Over-apologising and self-criticising about testing draws too much attention to the glitch. Repeating the entire point very slowly can feel patronising. Asking "can you hear me" repeatedly is disruptive and accomplishes nothing.'),
mcq(4,'Near the end of the call, Helen asks a question you are not fully prepared to answer with specific numbers. How do you handle this?',
'"That\'s a great question — I want to give you an accurate figure rather than estimate. I\'ll pull that exact number and send it to you this afternoon."',
['Make up an approximate figure and hope she does not notice later.','Say, "I\'m not sure — that\'s a good question though!"','Ask her to repeat the question while you frantically search your notes.'],
'Committing to a specific and timely follow-up is far better than an inaccurate answer. It demonstrates honesty and follow-through — both critical for a new client relationship.',
'Inventing a figure risks your credibility badly if the real number differs. "That\'s a good question" with no resolution is an empty non-answer. Asking her to repeat while visibly searching your notes signals disorganisation.'),
mcq(5,'The call ends well. Helen says, "Really enjoyed this — let\'s pick up the partnership conversation properly soon." How do you close the call?',
'"Absolutely — I\'ll send a summary of what we discussed and the figure you asked for this afternoon, and propose a few times for the next call. Great speaking with you, Helen."',
['"Yes, let\'s do it! I\'ll wait to hear from you."','"Fantastic — I\'ll set something up when I get a moment this week."','"Same — this was great. Talk soon!" and end the call immediately.'],
'Closing with a concrete action (summary, figure, proposed times) and a warm farewell wraps the call professionally and shows Helen exactly what to expect next.',
'Saying you\'ll wait to hear from her puts the initiative on her. Vaguely saying you\'ll set something up when you get a moment lacks commitment. A rushed "talk soon" close after a productive call feels anticlimactic.')],
'response':resp('You managed a professional video call with care — warm opening, respectful of her time, handled a tech glitch gracefully, and closed with a concrete next step. Now type or say aloud the follow-up email you would send Helen within two hours of the call ending.',
'Excellent! Professional video call etiquette is a core modern skill. You handled every phase — open, middle, disruption, and close — with the kind of calm, purposeful composure that leaves clients feeling confident in you.')})

levels.append({'id':16,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Group Conversation Skills',
'scenario':'You are at a dinner party with eight people you know to varying degrees. The group conversation has naturally split into pairs, leaving one person — Marcus — on the edge without an obvious entry point. You are in the main group and you notice Marcus looking slightly disconnected. You want to bring him in gracefully.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'Marcus is on the periphery of the conversation looking slightly disconnected. What is the most natural way to bring him in?',
'During a brief pause, turn to him and say, "Marcus, you know a lot about this — what\'s your take?"',
['Loudly announce, "Hey everyone, Marcus hasn\'t said much — let\'s hear from him."','Wait for Marcus to find his own entry point — it is not your responsibility.','Stop the conversation and say, "Actually Marcus, why don\'t you lead the next topic?"'],
'A direct but natural invitation that references his specific knowledge makes the inclusion feel earned rather than charitable, and gives him a clear, comfortable entry point.',
'Announcing that Marcus hasn\'t said much draws attention to his exclusion in a way that may embarrass him. Waiting indefinitely leaves the problem unsolved. Suddenly handing him the reins of the conversation without warning is too much pressure.'),
mcq(2,'Marcus responds but speaks quietly and the group does not quite hear him. How do you help?',
'Lean in, listen fully, then say, "That\'s a great point — Marcus, do you want to repeat that for the group? I think people will want to hear it."',
['Talk over him since he was not loud enough.','Say, "Marcus, speak up a bit — we can\'t hear you."','Paraphrase what you think he said without fully hearing it.'],
'Fully hearing his point, validating it, and inviting him to share it more widely is a kind amplification move that keeps his dignity intact.',
'Talking over him makes his entry point worse. Telling him to speak up is technically correct but slightly sharp. Paraphrasing something you half-heard risks misrepresenting what he actually said.'),
mcq(3,'Marcus has now made his point and the group is engaged. Someone starts talking over him before he finishes. How do you handle this?',
'Hold up a brief hand to the interrupter and say, "Hold on — I think Marcus was still making his point. Marcus?"',
['Let the interrupter take over — the conversation has momentum now.','Jump in yourself to finish Marcus\'s point for him.','Loudly say, "Excuse me — Marcus was speaking!"'],
'A calm, firm signal to the interrupter and an invitation back to Marcus protects his space without making the interrupter feel attacked.',
'Letting the interrupter take over reinforces the exact dynamic you were trying to fix. Finishing Marcus\'s point for him removes his own voice. A loud "excuse me" creates confrontation where a gentle redirect would suffice.'),
mcq(4,'Marcus finishes his point and the group responds positively. He looks noticeably more comfortable. How do you build on this moment?',
'Continue the conversation naturally, occasionally weaving Marcus in when relevant — treating him as a fully integrated member of the group rather than a project.',
['Keep directing every question at Marcus now that he is engaged.','Tell the group, "See — I told you Marcus had good things to add!"','Ask Marcus privately if he is feeling more comfortable, interrupting the flow to check in.'],
'Integrating Marcus naturally and proportionally is the goal — not making him the sole focus or treating his inclusion as an ongoing effort. He should now simply be part of the conversation.',
'Directing every question at him swings too far and can feel like a spotlight. Commenting on how you knew he had good things to say is self-congratulatory. Interrupting the group to privately check in on him draws attention to his earlier disconnection.'),
mcq(5,'Later in the evening Marcus quietly says, "Thanks for bringing me in earlier — I was starting to feel invisible." How do you respond?',
'"I noticed and I\'m glad it worked. You had really good things to say — the conversation was better for it."',
['"Oh, it was nothing — you would have found your way in eventually."','"Of course! That\'s just what you do — always include everyone."','"Ha, yes — you did look a bit lost for a moment there!"'],
'Accepting his thanks warmly and affirming the genuine value of his contributions makes the moment complete and meaningful without being either self-deprecating or self-praising.',
'Dismissing it as "nothing" understates a moment that clearly mattered to him. Claiming "that\'s just what you do" can sound hollow and formulaic. Joking that he looked lost is unkind and undoes the warmth of the whole evening.')],
'response':resp('You actively included a disconnected member of a group conversation, protected his space when he was interrupted, and gracefully integrated him without making it a production. Now type or say aloud what you would say to the host at the end of the evening about what made the dinner conversation work so well.',
'Superb! Managing group conversations with inclusion and awareness is a high-level social skill. You made the space better for everyone — and Marcus will remember that long after the dinner is over.')})

levels.append({'id':17,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Handling Awkward Silences',
'scenario':'You are on a first date at a nice restaurant. The conversation has been going well, but after a funny story you told, both of you reach for your water glass at the same time and there is suddenly a completely silent pause. It stretches on for a beat too long and you can feel the air change. You need to recover this gracefully.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'There is an awkward silence after a moment of laughter. What is the best first move?',
'Let the silence breathe for just one more second, then smile and say, "Okay — your turn. Tell me something surprising about yourself."',
['Fill the silence immediately with nervous chatter about anything that comes to mind.','Look at your phone to check the time.','Say, "Sorry — I\'m not sure what to say next!"'],
'Letting the silence breathe very briefly removes the panic, and then a playful invitation to them shifts the conversation forward in a warm, light way.',
'Nervous chatter to fill silence rarely produces anything meaningful and can make both people more anxious. Looking at your phone signals disconnection. Verbally flagging that you don\'t know what to say amplifies the awkwardness unnecessarily.'),
mcq(2,'They laugh at your question and seem to be thinking. How do you hold this comfortable moment?',
'Smile and wait with genuine curiosity — let them think without rushing them.',
['Jump in with a joke while they think to fill the pause.','Say, "Take your time — no rush," which can actually increase pressure.','Start answering your own question to give them more time.'],
'Sitting comfortably in the pause while they think communicates confidence and genuine interest in their answer. Silence while someone reflects is not awkward — it is anticipation.',
'Jumping in with a joke interrupts their thought process. "Take your time" can ironically highlight the fact that there\'s a pause. Answering your own question first takes the surprise out of theirs.'),
mcq(3,'They share something genuinely surprising — they were a competitive chess player in their teens. How do you respond in a way that makes the recovery from the earlier silence feel completely forgotten?',
'"A chess player — I genuinely did not see that coming. What happened — did you quit or just leave it behind?"',
['"Oh interesting! I\'m terrible at chess — I could never focus long enough."','"Wow — you must be really strategic then. Does that show up in how you work?"','"Really? I feel like I know so little about you still."'],
'Expressing genuine surprise and asking a specific follow-up about their chess journey keeps the energy alive and flowing — the silence is now completely irrelevant.',
'Immediately pivoting to yourself makes it about you. Projecting chess strategy onto their work personality can feel presumptuous. "I know so little about you" is accurate but creates an odd undercurrent of pressure.'),
mcq(4,'The conversation has fully recovered and is flowing beautifully. A second silence occurs naturally after a particularly deep exchange. How do you treat this one?',
'Let it settle naturally without rushing to fill it — sometimes silence after something meaningful is the best punctuation.',
['Immediately fill it the same way you filled the first one.','Say, "Sorry — we keep going quiet!"','Ask the waiter for the dessert menu to move things along.'],
'Not every silence needs fixing. A silence after a genuine, connected exchange is actually a sign of comfort and depth — learning to sit in it gracefully is a sign of conversational maturity.',
'Treating every silence as an emergency creates a frenetic pace. Commenting again on the silences draws self-conscious attention to them. Using the dessert menu as an escape hatch signals you are uncomfortable rather than at ease.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the evening, your date says, "I really enjoyed this — you are so easy to talk to." How do you receive this?',
'"That\'s a wonderful thing to hear — I felt the same way. Tonight went by really fast."',
['"Easy to talk to? You should see me with people I\'m nervous around!"','"Thanks — I had a few moments of panic but I think we recovered well!"','"Honestly, you\'re easy to talk to too — I usually struggle on first dates."'],
'Receiving the compliment gracefully, returning the sentiment, and noting how the time flew closes the evening on a warm, confident note.',
'Joking about being nervous undermines the impression you created. Referencing "moments of panic" replays the awkward silence rather than leaving it behind. Admitting you usually struggle on first dates frames the success as an exception rather than who you are.')],
'response':resp('You turned an awkward silence into a conversation springboard, recovered the date\'s energy, and let a second deeper silence settle naturally. Now type or say aloud the message you would send after you get home that evening.',
'Wonderful! Awkward silences are only awkward if you make them so. You showed that confidence and light curiosity are the best tools for recovery — and that some silences are actually worth keeping.')})

levels.append({'id':18,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Navigating Social Situations',
'scenario':'You are at a work colleague\'s birthday celebration in a restaurant — a mix of work friends and people you do not know. You arrive slightly late to find that seats are taken in a way that splits you from the people you know best, and you are seated between two strangers. You decide to make the most of it.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You sit down between two people you have never met. Both are already in conversation with others. How do you settle in and signal openness?',
'Settle your things, smile warmly at both neighbours when there is a natural moment, and say, "Hi, I\'m [Name] — I don\'t think we\'ve met."',
['Wait for one of them to introduce themselves to you first.','Pull out your phone and scroll until a conversation naturally starts.','Immediately try to catch the eye of your friends across the table.'],
'A proactive, warm introduction signals that you are comfortable in the situation and sets a positive tone for the table relationship.',
'Waiting passively for them to introduce themselves may take a long time. Scrolling your phone signals disengagement. Visibly trying to connect across the table to your friends signals that you would rather not be where you are.'),
mcq(2,'The person on your left, Anna, asks how you know the birthday person. How do you answer in a way that opens the door for conversation?',
'"We work together — she\'s one of those rare colleagues who\'s also become a real friend. How about you?"',
['"Oh, just from work." and leave it there.','Focus entirely on your own relationship without asking about hers.','Say, "I actually wasn\'t going to come but she insisted — I nearly made an excuse."'],
'A warm, brief answer with a specific personal quality and an immediate reciprocal question creates the natural back-and-forth of good conversation.',
'"Just from work" is minimal and gives Anna nothing to build on. Focusing entirely on your own relationship without asking about hers closes the reciprocal flow. Mentioning you almost did not come sends an odd message about your commitment to being there.'),
mcq(3,'You discover Anna is the birthday person\'s sister, who you have heard about but never met. How do you make this feel like a warm discovery?',
'"Oh — you\'re Anna! She has mentioned you so warmly. I\'ve been looking forward to meeting you without realising it was happening tonight."',
['"Oh wow — she talks about you quite a lot. There\'s a lot to live up to!"','"Wait — you\'re THE Anna? That\'s so funny — I had a completely different image of you."','"Oh I had no idea — she doesn\'t talk about family much, I suppose."'],
'Sharing that you heard about her warmly and expressing genuine delight at the serendipitous meeting makes the moment feel like a happy discovery and immediately builds warmth.',
'"A lot to live up to" is jokey but sets an odd competitive frame. "THE Anna" and commenting on a different image of her can feel a little strange. Suggesting the birthday person doesn\'t talk about family much may not be accurate and is a slightly cold opener.'),
mcq(4,'The dinner has progressed and you have been drawing Anna and the person on your right, Omar, into conversation with each other. How do you manage your role as the social bridge?',
'Look for connections between what Anna and Omar share and say, "Actually, Omar — Anna was just saying something related to that — you should hear her take."',
['Dominate the conversation between them by constantly inserting your own views.','Stop bridging and go quiet since they seem to be managing fine.','Ask the birthday person to come swap seats so you can sit near your friends.'],
'Finding a link between two people and explicitly connecting them is the highest-level group conversation skill — you become the catalyst for a new relationship forming.',
'Dominating a conversation you are trying to facilitate defeats the purpose. Withdrawing entirely when you have started the bridge can leave it half-built. Asking to swap seats at this point would be rude to both Anna and Omar.'),
mcq(5,'At the end of the evening, Anna says, "I\'m so glad I sat next to you tonight — you made this so easy." How do you respond?',
'"Honestly, same — I almost wished I was with my usual crowd, but I think I got the better end of the deal sitting here."',
['"You\'re too kind — I was just being friendly, that\'s all."','"Yes, I do tend to make people comfortable — it\'s something I\'ve always been good at."','"Oh good — I was a little nervous about sitting with strangers!"'],
'Receiving the warmth sincerely, admitting your initial concern, and framing the outcome as a genuine highlight is the perfect close — human, warm, and mutually honouring.',
'"Just being friendly" is deflecting. Immediately attributing it to a personal skill you\'ve always had sounds boastful. Admitting you were nervous about strangers can retroactively make the evening feel like an ordeal rather than a delight.')],
'response':resp('You turned an unplanned seating arrangement into a set of genuine new connections, actively bridged two strangers into conversation, and left as the social catalyst of the table. Now type or say aloud a brief message you would send the birthday person the following day mentioning what you appreciated about the evening.',
'Wonderful! Navigating unplanned social situations with openness and skill is one of the most valuable conversational abilities you can develop. You turned a potential inconvenience into one of the highlights of the night.')})

levels.append({'id':19,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Keeping Conversations Balanced',
'scenario':'You are in a long phone call with your old friend Jae. You realise about fifteen minutes in that you have been doing nearly all the talking — catching up on your own news — and Jae has been quietly listening, giving brief responses. You want to shift the balance and make the call genuinely two-way.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'You realise you have been talking about yourself for fifteen minutes and Jae has barely had a chance to share. What is the best way to course-correct?',
'Pause naturally and say, "Okay — I feel like I\'ve been monopolising this entire call. What\'s going on with you? Really."',
['Keep talking and assume Jae will jump in when they have something to say.','Apologise profusely: "I\'m so sorry — I\'ve been going on and on, that was so self-centred of me."','Ask a closed question: "So are you still at the same job?"'],
'A warm, self-aware pause followed by a genuine open invitation shifts the balance naturally without making the correction feel like a big deal.',
'Assuming Jae will jump in if they want to misses that some people are naturally quieter and need an invitation. Over-apologising makes the correction into a production and can make Jae feel obligated to reassure you. A closed yes-or-no question produces a minimal answer.'),
mcq(2,'Jae says, "Oh I\'m fine — nothing much to report really." How do you gently push past this deflection?',
'"Come on — there\'s always something. What\'s been taking up the most space in your head lately?"',
['"Oh okay, well anyway — I also wanted to tell you about..." and return to your own news.','"Nothing much? That sounds peaceful. I wish I had less going on!"','"Is everything okay? You seem a bit quiet."'],
'A light, curious push with an open, specific question — what\'s taking up space in your head — gives Jae an accessible, personal entry point that does not require them to have exciting news.',
'Returning to your own news immediately abandons the attempt entirely. Comparing their "nothing much" to your own busyness makes it about you again. "You seem quiet" is observationally accurate but can feel probing and put Jae on the spot.'),
mcq(3,'Jae opens up and starts sharing about a difficult situation at home. You are tempted to jump in with a solution you can already see. How do you resist this?',
'Hold the solution in your mind and instead ask, "That sounds really hard — how long has this been going on?"',
['Share the solution as soon as you see it: "Actually, here\'s what I think you should do..."','Say, "I went through something similar — let me tell you what worked for me."','Say, "Have you thought about talking to someone professionally about this?"'],
'Asking a follow-up question that deepens understanding before offering any advice keeps the conversation in listening mode and signals that you care about their experience, not just the resolution.',
'Jumping to a solution before fully hearing the situation can feel dismissive. Pivoting to your own similar experience redirects the conversation yet again. Suggesting professional help immediately, before they\'ve even fully shared, can feel deflecting.'),
mcq(4,'Jae has shared a lot and seems to feel heard. They then ask for your honest opinion. How do you give it in a way that is useful and balanced?',
'"My honest take — and tell me if this misses something — is that you might need to have a direct conversation with them rather than waiting for them to notice. But what does your gut say?"',
['"Honestly? You just need to leave the situation — I\'ve thought this since you started talking."','"I really don\'t know — it\'s your life, you\'ll figure it out."','"It\'s complicated. There are good and bad options. Hard to say really."'],
'Offering a specific, honest view while framing it as tentative and inviting their gut reaction balances directness with respect for their autonomy.',
'Announcing a conclusion you formed before they finished sharing undermines your listening. "You\'ll figure it out" is empty when they explicitly asked for your opinion. Vague fence-sitting does not serve someone who asked for honesty.'),
mcq(5,'The call ends with Jae saying, "This was really helpful — I feel much better." How do you close?',
'"I\'m glad. And I realised I had the floor the whole first half of this call — I\'m sorry about that. Thanks for sticking with me."',
['"Good — that\'s what friends are for!"','"Of course — I always give good advice."','"Great! Same time next month?"'],
'Acknowledging the imbalance at the close is a gracious act of honesty that tells Jae you noticed and care about the quality of the friendship.',
'"That\'s what friends are for" is warm but generic. Claiming you always give good advice turns Jae\'s gratitude into a credential. Immediately scheduling the next call without acknowledging the quality of this one feels routine when the moment deserves more.')],
'response':resp('You caught yourself monopolising the conversation, course-corrected warmly, drew Jae out with open questions, listened without jumping to solutions, and gave an honest view when asked. Now type or say aloud the text you would send Jae the following morning to check in on how they are feeling about what they shared.',
'Excellent! Keeping conversations balanced is an ongoing act of attention and generosity. You showed that noticing the imbalance and correcting it honestly is itself a form of care — and Jae felt that.')})

levels.append({'id':20,'journey_id':2,'journey_title':'Everyday Conversations','title':'Wrapping Up Conversations',
'scenario':'You are at the end of a long and genuinely enjoyable dinner with two friends — Bea and Theo. It is getting late and you have an early morning, but you do not want to be the one to end the evening on a flat note. You want to close the night in a way that feels complete, warm, and leaves everyone looking forward to the next time.',
'mcqs':[
mcq(1,'It is getting late and you need to head home but the conversation is still going. How do you signal the close without killing the energy?',
'During a natural lull, stretch slightly and say, "I have to be honest — I have a brutal early start tomorrow, but this has been one of those evenings I genuinely didn\'t want to end."',
['Start gathering your things without saying anything and see if they notice.','Say, "Right — time to go!" and start putting on your coat.','Ask the waiter for the bill on behalf of everyone without checking with them.'],
'Acknowledging your need to leave while sincerely expressing how much you enjoyed the evening honours both the constraint and the connection.',
'Gathering your things silently puts the group in an awkward guessing game. A blunt "right, time to go" is abrupt and loses the warmth of the evening. Ordering the bill for everyone without asking assumes agreement and removes their agency.'),
mcq(2,'Bea says, "Aw, already? Okay — just five more minutes!" How do you handle this affectionately without caving or being harsh?',
'Laugh and say, "Five minutes I can do — what\'s the one thing we haven\'t covered yet?"',
['Cave entirely and say, "Okay, maybe another hour then."','Say, "I really can\'t — I\'m sorry" and stand up.','Say, "The five-minute rule never works — let\'s just say goodbye properly."'],
'Playfully accepting the five minutes and giving it a purpose — one more thing — creates a fun, intentional close rather than a negotiation.',
'Caving entirely when you genuinely need to go creates resentment for yourself. Standing up abruptly while apologising feels cold. Preemptively calling out the five-minute rule as ineffective is a bit pedantic.'),
mcq(3,'In the final five minutes, Theo raises a genuinely interesting new topic. How do you engage with it without letting it derail your exit?',
'"That is such a good thread — let\'s save it for next time and give it the time it deserves. I don\'t want to rush something interesting."',
['Dive into the topic fully and let the five minutes become thirty.','Say, "I can\'t get into that now — maybe another time."','Give a quick, surface-level answer just to close it off.'],
'Acknowledging the value of the topic and consciously deferring it to next time is a graceful way to honour both your timeline and the quality of the upcoming conversation.',
'Fully diving in undoes the exit you were gracefully executing. A flat "not now" is dismissive of something Theo clearly wanted to discuss. A rushed surface answer does not serve a topic that deserves more.'),
mcq(4,'You stand up to leave. Bea says, "This was honestly such a good evening." How do you close the night with both of them at once?',
'"It really was — you two make every dinner feel like a highlight. Same time next month?"',
['"Yes, it was — I\'m glad I came, I was a bit tired when I left the house."','"It was great! I\'ll text you both in the group chat."','"Good evening, good company — can\'t ask for more." and put on your coat.'],
'Reflecting the warmth of the evening back to them personally and anchoring a next time closes the night with both closure and anticipation.',
'Mentioning you were tired when you left the house is a minor deflection that slightly undercuts the sincerity. "I\'ll text in the group chat" is fine but slightly impersonal for such a warm close. A generic platitude while putting on your coat is an abrupt ending.'),
mcq(5,'Outside on the street, the three of you hug and there is a moment before you all go your separate ways. How do you handle the very last goodbye?',
'"Goodnight, you two — this is exactly the kind of evening I need more of. Go safe." and mean it as you walk away.',
['Linger awkwardly, unsure of whether to say anything more.','Say one more long thing and restart a mini conversation on the street.','Give a quick wave and turn away while they are still saying goodbye.'],
'A final line that is warm, specific to the evening\'s spirit, and wishes them well closes the night on exactly the right note — memorable without being extended.',
'Lingering without saying anything creates the uncomfortable door-goodbye effect. Starting a new conversation on the street undoes the close entirely. Turning away while they are still speaking is dismissive of the people who just spent the evening with you.')],
'response':resp('You closed a wonderful evening with grace, warmth, and a clear sense of completion — without killing the energy or dragging it out. Now type or say aloud the message you would send Bea and Theo the following morning to affirm that the evening was as good as it felt.',
'Beautiful! Wrapping up conversations well is a form of respect for the time and energy people have invested. Your close was warm, honest, and left everyone looking forward to next time — which is the best possible ending.')})
